{
  "subtype": "predictive_feature_engineering",
  "focus_variable": "department",
  "per_column": [
    {
      "name": "primary_reason",
      "role": "predictor",
      "is_predictor": true,
      "rationale": "This is a normalized/extracted version of the reason_for_visit, capturing the chief complaint (e.g., 'well-child visit', 'sports physical', 'diabetes follow-up'). The visit reason lies outside the department and is precisely the signal the query targets for pointing to departments. It is knowable at intake (pre-outcome), so no leakage. Its main weakness is high cardinality/near-uniqueness, but semantically it carries strong predictive signal about department."
    },
    {
      "name": "condition_category",
      "role": "predictor",
      "is_predictor": true,
      "rationale": "A rolled-up clinical category of the visit reason (Preventive_Care, Musculoskeletal_Issue, Cardiovascular_Issue, etc.). This is a derived predictor from the reason text, external to department, available at intake, and generalizes the reason into a low-cardinality feature that plausibly maps strongly to departments. Legitimate, high-utility predictor."
    }
  ],
  "n_predictors": 2,
  "predictive_utility": 0.75,
  "rationale": "Both added columns are legitimate, non-leaking predictors of department derived from the visit reason, directly matching the analytical intent of finding which reasons point to which departments. condition_category is especially useful: it converts the free-text reason into a compact, generalizable categorical feature that a model can leverage. primary_reason adds signal but its extreme cardinality limits standalone leverage. As a set they add meaningful predictive leverage beyond the raw reason_for_visit text, though they are transformations of an existing column rather than genuinely new external variables, capping the incremental utility."
}

